Z||000003||rogergarrison||06-02-2001||04:13 PM||||coat it with lots of vaseline or a layer of saran wrap, then pile on some paper masche.||||reg||1
Z||000004||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-02-2001||04:15 PM||||Thanks for the tip  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>coat it with lots of vaseline or a layer of saran wrap, then pile on some paper masche.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||AusFiero||06-02-2001||08:51 PM||||arrrrggghh Vaseline?<P>The correct reply would be to give the panel you want to mould off about 6 sequences of polishing it with fibreglass mould release wax. Then when it is polished to a high gloss use a sponge to apply a thin layer of Poly Vinyl Acetate (PVA).<P>When this drys use a good quality tooling gelcoat and apply it to the manufacturers recommended thickness and catylist to gelcoat ratio. Do not make it go off to fast or to slow as it will either cause shrinkage and pre-release before it is layed up, or it will cause tripe, a horrible little pattern through your laminate that feels like scales.<P>After that is done start you laminating when the gelcoat is off. Do not leave it lieing around for days gelcoated. Apply 1 layer of fibreglass tissue and a layer of 225 gram fibreglass mat then roll out all your air bubbles. After this goes off it is time to make the mould thicker and reinforce it. Apply 4 layers 450 gram fibreglass mat (2layers roll air out, leave to dry, repeat process). <P>When this has gone off apply any strengthening ribs you may need to your mould and glass then on using the above methods.<P>Now when this is cured you can pop your mould off the scoop and it shouldn't need a great deal of preparation other than a wipe down with hot water to clean it up. Then wax and PVA as above. PVA only the first time a new mould is used then clean it off and rewax.<P>Then follow the processes above to make your product using 2 layers of 450 gram mat with an extra layer around the edges.<P>Also greentrim all your fibreglass work when you are making it. Is better than having to grind and cut later.<P>That is a very basic rundown on the process. I spent several years making race car bodies, panels and custom car stuff from scratch so that is just some of much I learn't in those years. Don't expect good results straight off if you have never done it before.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||14
